  "title": "Age-related preference for geometric spatial cues during real-world navigation",
  "authors": "Marcia B\u00e9cu et al., 2020",
  "research_focus": "This experimental study investigated how geometry and landmark cues control navigation in young and older adults in a real, ecological environment, recording body and gaze dynamics to characterize information-encoding strategies and their link to age-related navigation difficulties.",
  "sample_context": {
    "n_participants": 39,
    "description": "20 young adults (range, 19-37 years, mean=26.25, 11 females, 9 males) and 19 older adults (range, 61-81 years, mean=72.2, 10 females, 9 males). Participants were clinically and functionally screened for normal visual, audio-vestibular, postural, oculomotor, and cognitive (visuo-spatial memory) characteristics. Recruited from the SilverSight cohort population.",
    "setting": "A real street-like, open-field maze (Streetlab platform, Vision Institute) with a rectangular enclosure (8.55x4.30 m\u00b2) and wall panels imitating street scenery."
  },
