  "supporting_quotes_or_evidence_snippets": [],
  "derived_hypotheses": [
    "If the age-induced deficit related to cue preference is indeed true, it can be targeted by precisely tuned rehabilitation techniques and design of informative environments.",
    "Making the geometry of an experimental environment unambiguous will improve the use of allocentric strategies in older adults, contrary to the proposal that the use of allocentric strategies is impaired in ageing.",
    "In an environment presenting both landmark and geometric cues, navigation performance of children and older adults, but not young adults, should improve under a condition of polarized geometry compared to a non-polarized one (keeping the landmark array constant across the two conditions)."
  ],
  "mechanism_candidates": [
    "The observed stronger preference for geometric cues in ageing is caused by an impaired capacity to infer position (either of the navigator per se or of the goal) relative to a constellation of landmarks.",
    "The longer reorientation period of older adults was associated with a more repetitive sampling of visual cues, reflecting a suboptimal visual scanning policy.",
    "This suboptimal visual scanning policy could reflect either a reduction in the area from which information can be extracted, requiring additional ocular fixations, or deficits of behavioural monitoring or impaired memory processes.",
    "The ability to combine both cue types, as observed in young adults, deteriorates with age to a differing degree in different subjects.",
    "The capacity to efficiently use landmarks for navigation improves in early development, but deteriorates with ageing, giving rise to an inverted U-shaped dependence on landmark processing across the lifespan."
  ],
  "transferability_limits": [
    "The size of an experimental environment can affect the use of visual cues for navigation, suggesting findings might be size-dependent."
  ],
